before:
Here is a complete choice between two pets and a fallback.
```javascript
const pet = "cat";
let message = "";
if (pet === "dog") {
  message = "Time for a walk";
} else if (pet === "cat") {
  message = "Time for a game";
} else {
  message = "Ask what this pet needs";
}
console.log(message);
```
The dog condition is false. The cat condition is then checked and is true. That block assigns the message, and the final line prints `Time for a game`.

Unknown input needs a useful answer too.
```javascript
const pet = "lizard";
let message = "";
if (pet === "dog") {
  message = "Time for a walk";
} else if (pet === "cat") {
  message = "Time for a game";
} else {
  message = "Ask what this pet needs";
}
console.log(message);
```
Both comparisons are false, so this prints `Ask what this pet needs`. The fallback prevents the message from remaining empty when the value is unexpected.

